@* A small floating glossary orb shown on the Saints browse and detail pages.
    It matches the shared floating-circle family (back-to-top, prayer guide, Summa guide, liturgical orb):
    a bottom-right circle that slides up to stack above the back-to-top arrow.
    Tapping it opens a Dialog explaining the saint facets &mdash; states of life, canonizations, eras,
    and patronages &mdash; so the terms used on the cards and detail pages are self-explanatory. *@

<FloatingGuide Title="Reading the saints" Eyebrow="What the labels mean" AriaLabel="Glossary: what the saint labels mean" Tooltip="What the labels mean" DialogClass="dialog--glossary" Raised="Raised">
    <Icon>
        <svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
             stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
            <path d="M7 7.6589C6.36815 7.18374 6 6.61337 6 6C6 4.34315 8.68629 3 12 3C15.3137 3 18 4.34315 18 6C18 6.61337 17.6318 7.18374 17 7.6589M21 21H17.5M21 21L19.8842 18.7683C19.7434 18.4869 19.6731 18.3461 19.6156 18.2055C19.2935 17.4168 19.2316 16.5459 19.439 15.7195C19.4759 15.5722 19.5257 15.423 19.6252 15.1244L19.9211 14.2368C20.3884 12.8347 19.1238 11.4691 17.69 11.8275C17.2341 11.9415 16.8037 12.1402 16.4213 12.4134L13.3007 14.6509M21 21H17M3 21H6.5M3 21L4.11583 18.7683C4.25657 18.4869 4.32695 18.3461 4.38437 18.2055C4.7065 17.4168 4.76839 16.5459 4.56104 15.7195C4.52408 15.5722 4.47432 15.423 4.3748 15.1244L4.07893 14.2368C3.61157 12.8347 4.87622 11.4691 6.31001 11.8275C6.76593 11.9415 7.19633 12.1402 7.57875 12.4134L10.6993 14.6509M3 21H7M13.3007 14.6509C13.7125 15.0824 14.1347 15.8424 14.7973 17.0351L17 21M13.3007 14.6509C13.145 14.4877 12.9907 14.3714 12.8255 14.2966C12.3009 14.0588 11.6991 14.0588 11.1745 14.2966C11.0093 14.3714 10.855 14.4877 10.6993 14.6509M10.6993 14.6509C10.2875 15.0824 9.86531 15.8424 9.2027 17.0351L7 21M17 21H7M14 9C14 10.1046 13.1046 11 12 11C10.8954 11 10 10.1046 10 9C10 7.89543 10.8954 7 12 7C13.1046 7 14 7.89543 14 9Z" />
        </svg>
    </Icon>
    <ChildContent>
        <div class="glossary">
            <p>
                Each saint is tagged with a few plain labels so you can browse and compare them. Here is
                what those labels mean.
            </p>

            <GuideSection Title="Era">
            <p>
                The era places a saint in the sweep of salvation history, from the patriarchs and
                prophets of the Old Testament to our own day.
            </p>
            <GuideList>
                <GuideItem Term="Old Testament">the holy men and women of
                    Israel before Christ &mdash; the patriarchs, prophets, and kings such as David and
                    Solomon &mdash; whom the Church honors as saints of the old covenant.</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="Early Church">the apostolic age through the
                    Church Fathers and the great councils, roughly the first six centuries (up to about
                    600 AD).</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="Medieval">the Middle Ages, roughly 600 to
                    1500: the age of monasticism, the mendicant orders, and the scholastics.</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="Early modern">roughly 1500 to 1800: the
                    Reformation, the Catholic Reformation, and the great missionary expansion.</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="Modern">roughly 1800 to the present, from
                    the industrial age through the Second Vatican Council and our own day.</GuideItem>
            </GuideList>
            </GuideSection>

            <GuideSection Title="State of life">
            <p>
                A saint's state of life is the vocation or office they lived. Holy orders build on one
                another &mdash; deacon, then priest, then bishop &mdash; so a saint often carries more
                than one. These labels are independent, and a saint may hold several.
            </p>
            <GuideList>
                <GuideItem Term="Religious">a vowed member of a religious
                    order or community (Franciscan, Carmelite, Benedictine, and so on), as distinct from
                    diocesan clergy.</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="Deacon">the first rank of Holy Orders,
                    ordained to service of the word, the altar, and charity.</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="Priest">ordained to offer the Mass,
                    forgive sins, and shepherd souls.</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="Bishop">a successor of the Apostles who
                    holds the fullness of Holy Orders and governs a local church.</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="Pope">the Bishop of Rome and successor of
                    St. Peter, who leads the whole Church. (An office, not a separate order.)</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="Lay faithful">a baptized member of the
                    Church who is not ordained, living holiness in the world.</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="Martyr">one who was killed for the faith,
                    bearing the ultimate witness to Christ.</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="Virgin">one who consecrated their virginity
                    to God for the sake of the Kingdom.</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="Widow">one who, after the death of a
                    spouse, gave the rest of their life to God's service.</GuideItem>
            </GuideList>
            </GuideSection>

            <GuideSection Title="Canonization">
            <p>
                Canonization is the Church's path to formally recognizing that a person is in heaven and
                may be honored publicly. These labels mark how far that recognition has advanced.
            </p>
            <GuideList>
                <GuideItem Term="Servant of God">the first step, given once
                    the Church opens an official cause for the person.</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="Venerable">declared to have lived heroic
                    virtue, though not yet beatified.</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="Beatified">declared "Blessed," permitting
                    limited public devotion (usually after a verified miracle).</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="Canonized">declared a saint, honored by the
                    whole Church with the title "Saint."</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="Doctor of the Church">a saint recognized for
                    the outstanding value of their teaching for the whole Church.</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="Pre-Congregation">honored as a saint by
                    ancient tradition, from before the modern canonization process existed.</GuideItem>
            </GuideList>
            </GuideSection>

            <GuideSection Title="Patronage">
            <p>
                A patron saint is a special heavenly intercessor and protector for a cause, place, trade,
                or group of people &mdash; often tied to something in the saint's own life. Patronages
                are a time-honored way of asking a particular saint to pray for a particular need; they
                are shown on each profile to help you find a saint close to your situation.
            </p>
            </GuideSection>
        </div>
    </ChildContent>
    <Footer>
        <button type="button" class="btn" @onclick="context.CloseAsync">Close</button>
    </Footer>
</FloatingGuide>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// When true, the orb sits one slot higher so it clears another floating control that already
    /// occupies the base slot (e.g. the liturgical orb on the Saints browse page).
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Raised { get; set; }
}

